03:05:49,934 --> 03:05:55,780
So this is maybe also something that you would like to do to maybe avoid unnecessary preloading.

2175
03:05:56,814 --> 03:06:01,504
Also, some other options that we can set here are options related to caching.

2176
03:06:01,902 --> 03:06:06,944
So when we preload a route, so let's say let's refresh here, let's maybe hover over about.

2177
03:06:08,878 --> 03:06:10,544
Now, as you can see, it is preloaded.

2178
03:06:10,862 --> 03:06:16,452
If we hover over it again, it is not going to preload again until a certain amount of time has passed.

2179
03:06:16,782 --> 03:06:25,572
It is going to be cached for some time, and we can control this cache by using the default preload max age here, which is 30 seconds by default.

2180
03:06:25,744 --> 03:06:30,532
After 30 seconds, any cached preloaded routes are going to be removed.

2181
03:06:30,990 --> 03:06:36,132
And also we can control how long preloaded data is considered fresh

2182
03:06:36,814 --> 03:06:42,852
by using these options: default preload stale time in our create router when we define our router,

2183
03:06:43,790 --> 03:06:50,084
or preload stale time if we would like to specify this option for a specific route and not for all of our routes.

2184
03:06:50,670 --> 03:06:58,704
But let's not get too deep into this because actually in the next section we're going to talk about caching in general in 10 Stack Router.

2185
03:06:59,024 --> 03:07:08,804
As we've mentioned in 10 Stack Router we have built-in caching, and this caching can be enough for, as mentioned here, small to medium-sized application.

2186
03:07:09,390 --> 03:07:16,708
However, you can also integrate something like 10 stack query for a more advanced caching solution if you need that.

2187
03:07:17,422 --> 03:07:27,012
So if you are working on a project, make sure to take a look at maybe this page. here we have the pros and cons listed for the caching that comes with 10 stack router by default.

2188
03:07:27,310 --> 03:07:34,852
And so by reading this, you can probably decide if you can use this caching or you need to use something like 10 stack query with 10 stack router.

2189
03:07:35,726 --> 03:07:42,660
However, what we're going to do is we're going to discuss the built-in cache in 10 Stack Router. We're not going to use 10 Stack Query for this video.

2190
03:07:43,374 --> 03:07:48,676
So let's take a look at this default caching. Let's go back to our application and let's maybe

2191
03:07:49,584 --> 03:07:51,908
let's actually keep here in this post route.

2192
03:07:52,462 --> 03:07:56,132
And let's say the post is going to take some time to load, for example.

2193
03:07:56,718 --> 03:08:04,260
So we can go to our post, post ID route, and we can make this a little bit slower by maybe in our loader function in here.

2194
03:08:04,718 --> 03:08:07,744
Let's add some artificial delay in here.

2195
03:08:09,198 --> 03:08:09,664
Like so.

2196
03:08:10,926 --> 03:08:12,932
So now let's maybe give it a refresh.

2197
03:08:13,198 --> 03:08:17,412
If we visit any post, it is going to take some time to load.

2198
03:08:18,158 --> 03:08:20,544
However, after loading, it is going to be cached.

2199
03:08:20,750 --> 03:08:26,532
So if we go back and visit the same post again, as you can see, it did not take any time to load.

2200
03:08:27,214 --> 03:08:35,184
So in 10 Stack Router, we have this stale while revalidate caching by default, which means if we have something in the cache,

2201
03:08:35,344 --> 03:08:41,284
we're going to serve it and then refresh our data in the background. So if we take a look at our network here, let's clear.

2202
03:08:42,862 --> 03:08:44,612
Let's go back and clear our network.

2203
03:08:45,166 --> 03:08:47,108
If we visit this post again,

2204
03:08:48,078 --> 03:08:50,116
as you can see, we got something instantly.

2205
03:08:50,414 --> 03:08:59,600
However, the load function still ran and after three seconds we got our requests here. So even though we got something from the cache, we still refresh our data every time.

2206
03:08:59,760 --> 03:09:04,320
And this is referred to as stale while revalidate or swr.

2207
03:09:04,782 --> 03:09:07,040
But this cache, however, only lives in memory.

2208
03:09:07,360 --> 03:09:12,420
Once we refresh our page, it is going to be gone. So let's go back, for example, refresh.

2209
03:09:13,440 --> 03:09:17,828
And if we click on the same post again, now it's going to load again.

2210
03:09:18,254 --> 03:09:23,460
Nothing is going to be served from the cache because the cache is gone since we have refreshed our page.

2211
03:09:24,142 --> 03:09:29,444
And of course, we have some options to play around with the caching settings, how long should it live, and so on.

2212
